in this video we'll talk about  neutrophil which is a very important  immune cell  neutrophils are most abundant leukocytes  they are component of the innate immune  system neutrophils play crucial roles  against  bacteria neutrophils are highly  phagocytotic and neutrophil levels  increase in the blood when there is an  infection in our body if you haven't yet  subscribed the channel hit that  subscribe button and stay tuned till the  end  so neutrophils are the most abundant  leukocytes and they are the majority of  the leukocyte population they constitute  around 50 to 70 percent of the total  circulating leukocytes  now other than neutrophils the basophils  and eosinophils they are kind of the  minority  so now we understand neutrophils are  damn important for the body and we'll  try to understand why they are important  before that let's talk about the  development of the neutrophils  neutrophils are born in the bone marrow  and they are derived from the myeloid  progenitor lineage  from myeloid progenitors neutrophils  develop in a subsequent step and then  they get secreted into the bloodstream  in the circulation they can survive  several hours and then eventually they  move into the tissue space  now in the tissue space they play  several function in the tissue space  they also die they fight the pathogen so  lot of actions happen in the tissue  space  now let's look at the development of the  neutrophils in bit more details  so from the  myeloblast neutrophil develops in  different stages  it becomes  pro myelocyte myelocyte metamyelocyte  band cells and eventually they become  mature neutrophils you must be wondering  what is the difference in between these  cell types or this  transient cellular states  one point that differentiate between  this cellular  transition state  is the presence of granules  so primary or azurophilic granules are  common in many stages of these  neutrophil development whereas  secretory granules and secondary  granules are more specific towards a  mature neutrophil  that means neutrophil has different type  of granules and they are one type of  granulocyte right  so neutrophils has a zero field granules  also known as primary granules which has  myeloperoxidase  natural serine proteases cathepsin g  protein s3 and many other enzymes  including lysozymes  there are specific granules which has  lactoferrin lysozymes cytochrome b558  collagenase  they also have gelatinous granule which  has cytochrome b558 lysozymes  cd11bnc8 and secretary granules which  has cr1 alkaline phosphatase  and  fmlpr  so this is a vivid description of the  heterogeneity of granules that possessed  by the neutrophil and all of these  secretory contents have different  functions and we would understand that  in a bit more details  so neutrophil invasion is the hallmark  feature of acute inflammation so imagine  you have a small cut in your hand in the  nearby area there would be some amount  of inflammation in a mirror in a minor  quantity and one of the hallmark feature  would be neutrophil in in  neutrophil innervation in that  particular area if you don't know what  is acute inflammation you can watch my  video on acute inflammation  but let's move on  let's say we are struck by a nail  and some of the bacteria that was there  in the rusty nail has entered our body  so neutrophils are the first responders  who can like kind of fade phagocytos  those bacteria and kind of fight them  also other cells such as the resident  macrophages dendritic cells they can  also eat up the pathogen and they can  coordinate with the neutrophils and the  way they coordinate is by secretion of  several cytokines this secretion of  several cytokines especially the  inflammatory cytokines  allows  different changes in the body  one of the change is expression of  surface adhesion molecule in the blood  vessels generally in the blood vessel  neutrophils are  circulates in a rolling fashion  now when there is adhesive expression in  the endothelial wall neutrophil slows  down  and gradually there are several  interactions with the neutrophil  and the  capillary wall and this results in a  slower rolling of neutrophil neutrophils  now slows down and there are certain  cytoskeletal rearrangement happens  in the neutrophil this kind of squeezes  the neutrophil through the narrow  capillary  the narrow gap between the capillary  endothelial cells and ultimately  neutrophil moves out of the blood vessel  and enters the tissue space this process  is also known as diabetes or neutrophil  extravasation  and this process is increased when you  have inflammation a lot of neutrophils  are getting signals from the inflam  inflamed region and they are attracting  more and more neutrophils towards it  neutrophil has entered the tissue space  right now  and now they can see some opponents the  bacteria and now their goal is to kill  those bacteria literally neutrophil  would chase that bacteria until they can  engulf the bacteria and phagocytose them  once phagocytosed that bacteria there  would be lysosomal fusion and the  lysosomal enzymes can actually degrade  the bacteria  alternatively neutrophil has elaborate  mechanism by which they can generate  reactive oxygen species inside the cell  and this reactive oxygen species can  kill and damage the membrane of bacteria  or any other kind of pathogen that has  been in  innervated the body  and thereby  neutrophil can eliminate pathogen  now neutrophil is super important for  the body and in diseases like chronic  granulomatous disease there are  granuloma formation  in chronic  in cgd or chronic  brand new low motors disease  the neutrophils lack the enzyme nadph  oxidase  in absence of this enzyme ros production  is kind of abrogated  and that is why the pathogen killing  efficiency of this neutrophil and many  other  phagocyte phagocytes are decreased and  that's why they kind of create a cluster  around these pathogens and this is known  as granuloma now we understand from a  clinical perspective why it is super  important  neutrophils also take part in antigen  antibody dependent cellular cytotoxicity  if you need to learn this particular  topic in details you can click on the i  button  anyway in short  our  body produces antibody against any kind  of invading pathogen let's say this  bacteria and all these cells including  neutrophils  has the receptors against the fc region  of the antibody  so ultimately they can  bind to these antibodies and engulf this  pathogen and thereby eliminating them  now phagocytosis by neutrophil is a  strong defense mechanism against fungal  infections not only neutrophil protects  our body against bacterial infection but  also for fungal infection they are also  efficient  neutrophil takes part in the type 3  hypersensitivity reaction which is an  immune complex mediated hypersensitivity  reaction  so in that case  immune complexes can circulate in the  blood vessels and somewhere they can  settle down in different locations in  the blood vessels  and that triggers neutrophil to  degranulate and this creates local  inflammation  in those regions and thereby it results  in the harmful effect of type 3  hypersensitivity reaction  anyway  neutrophils are also different in  different ages that means a fresh  neutrophil is very different from a aged  neutrophil in terms of surface  expression of molecules  the surface receptors that are expressed  by a fresh versus aged neutrophils are  widely different that means we can  understand they are really different  even they are mature  also the circulating neutrophils are  really different from one another and  there is a huge heterogeneity in the  neutrophil population depending on where  they end up that means whether they go  to the lung or let's say spleen or the  lymph node  they end up being a slightly different  neutrophil from their counterparts  so this is the latest research that  people are focusing on to identify the  heterogeneity within the neutrophil  population and try to understand whether  there are subgroups which play a  specific function  so in this video we learned several  things about neutrophil like their most  abundant leukocytes we looked at their  role in the immune system we looked at  how they phagocytosis bacteria and  ultimately how they can eliminate  bacteria with the help of ross we also  looked at what happens when neutrophils  ability is compromised and that results  in diseases like cgd  and we also looked at neuro neutrophil  tech parts in  type 3 hypersensitivity and there might  be different sub types of neutrophil in  our body so that kind of summarize the  whole video  all the lecture notes and  flashcards are available in my facebook  page so all the link for the facebook  page is provided in the description  please follow me and like me on facebook  you can also get several dynamic flash  cards for your revision in my facebook  page don't forget to like share and  subscribe you can support my channel on  patreon i'm present in wide variety of  social media feel free to connect i  would be happy to help my courses are  also present in unacademy which is  india's biggest online learning platform  using a code ap 10 you can get a 10  discount on the subscription

